.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt LS 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm ls
.Nd list directory contents
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl 1AaCcdFfGgHhikLlmnopqRrSsTtUuwx
.Op Fl -color Ns = Ns Ar when
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
For each
.Ar file
that is a directory,
.Nm
lists what it contains; any other
.Ar file
is listed itself.
Files are listed first, then directories, each group sorted on its own.
With no
.Ar file ,
the current directory is listed.
When there is more than one operand, each directory's listing is headed by its
name and a colon.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl 1
List one name per line.
This is the default when the output is not a terminal.
.It Fl A
List every entry but
.Pa \&.
and
.Pa .. .
Always on for the super-user.
.It Fl a
List every entry, those whose names begin with a dot included.
.It Fl C
List in columns, sorted down each column.
This is the default on a terminal.
.It Fl c
Use the time the file's status last changed, to show and to sort by.
.It Fl d
List a directory operand as itself, not its contents.
.It Fl F
After each name write
.Ql /
for a directory,
.Ql @
for a symbolic link,
.Ql *
for an executable,
.Ql =
for a socket and
.Ql |
for a FIFO.
.It Fl f
Don't sort; list in the order the directory holds.
Implies
.Fl a ,
and cancels
.Fl r ,
.Fl S
and
.Fl t .
.It Fl G
Colour the names by type, if the output is a terminal (see
.Sx ENVIRONMENT ) .
.It Fl g
As
.Fl l ,
but without the owner.
.It Fl H
Follow symbolic links named on the command line.
.It Fl h
Show sizes with a unit suffix (B, K, M, G, T, P, E), in at most four
characters.
.It Fl i
Write each file's inode number before it.
.It Fl k
Count
.Fl s
sizes in kilobytes (1024 bytes).
.It Fl L
Follow every symbolic link: list what it points to.
.It Fl l
Long format: for each file its type and mode, link count, owner, group, size
in bytes, time and name, and for a symbolic link
.Ql ->
and its contents.
A device's size is its major and minor number.
The time is shown as month, day, hour and minute if it is within six months
of now, else as month, day and year.
A directory's listing begins with
.Ql total
and the sum of its files' block counts.
.It Fl m
List across the page, separated by commas.
.It Fl n
As
.Fl l ,
but with owner and group as numbers.
.It Fl o
As
.Fl l ,
but without the group.
.It Fl P
Follow no symbolic link, command line or not.
.It Fl p
Write
.Ql /
after the name of a directory.
.It Fl q
Write each non-printing character as
.Ql \&? .
The default on a terminal.
.It Fl R
List subdirectories too, as they are met.
.It Fl r
Reverse the sort order.
.It Fl S
Sort by size, largest first.
.It Fl s
Write each file's size in blocks before it, in units of 512 bytes, or
.Ev BLOCKSIZE ,
or 1024 with
.Fl k .
.It Fl T
Show times in full: month, day, hour, minute, second and year.
.It Fl t
Sort by time, newest first.
.It Fl U
Use the time the file was created (the status change time where the file
system doesn't keep it), to show and to sort by.
.It Fl u
Use the time the file was last read, to show and to sort by.
.It Fl w
Write names as they are, non-printing characters included.
The default when the output is not a terminal.
.It Fl x
List in columns, sorted across each row.
.It Fl -color Ns = Ns Ar when
Colour names
.Cm always ,
.Cm never ,
or, with
.Cm auto ,
as
.Fl G
does.
.El
.Pp
Of
.Fl 1 ,
.Fl C ,
.Fl l ,
.Fl m
and
.Fl x ,
the last given counts; so does the last of
.Fl c ,
.Fl U
and
.Fl u .
.Fl S
beats
.Fl t .
Names are sorted by their bytes.
.Pp
A symbolic link named on the command line is followed unless
.Fl d ,
.Fl F
or
.Fl l
is given;
.Fl H
and
.Fl L
follow it anyway,
.Fl P
never.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width CLICOLOR_FORCE
.It Ev BLOCKSIZE
The unit for
.Fl s
sizes and the
.Ql total
line, such as
.Ql 1k .
.It Ev CLICOLOR
If set, colour as
.Fl G
does.
.It Ev CLICOLOR_FORCE
Colour even when the output is not a terminal.
.It Ev COLUMNS
The width to fill, ahead of the terminal's own.
.It Ev LSCOLORS
The colours, as FreeBSD's: eleven pairs of foreground and background for
directory, symbolic link, socket, FIFO, executable, block device, character
device, set-user-ID executable, set-group-ID executable, directory writable by
others with the sticky bit, and without it.
Each is
.Cm a
to
.Cm h
(black, red, green, brown, blue, magenta, cyan, grey), the same in
capitals for bold, or
.Cm x
for the default.
The default is
.Ql exfxcxdxbxegedabagacad .
.It Ev TZ
The time zone for times.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
The newest files first, with sizes people read:
.Dl $ ls -lht
.Pp
Everything under a directory, with types marked:
.Dl $ ls -RF /etc
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chmod 1 ,
.Xr stat 1 ,
.Xr hier 7
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq).
.Fl G ,
.Fl h ,
.Fl P ,
.Fl T ,
.Fl U ,
.Fl w
and
.Fl -color
are extensions, as in the BSDs.
.Fl g
and
.Fl o
are POSIX's (FreeBSD's
.Fl o
shows file flags instead).
.Sh HISTORY
An
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v1 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
